an update that only turns the camera zeroes it and a stale velocity lasts only until the next changed update (the lobby's own call repeats one value, so it makes a single update); QMixer's time-based tracker (option bit 8, 0x18009cf4) is not enabled by the listener record (flags 0 or 0x800, 0x6b9930); the request word +0xc has 0xc0 ORed in for every voice without voice flag 0x20000 (0x6bc2af; 0x40 maps to the no-Doppler bit through 0x6d22d0) and 2 ORed in when the sound system's flags 8 and 0x100 (reverb) are both set (0x6b9acb), which passes the test of a second EnableChannel (0x6c5690, +0xc & 0x1a), but that send runs synchronously inside the submission (the record 0x70a580 executes through 0x6b7f00 -> 0x6b8360) before the Play record (0x6c5b20 -> 0x6d2380) whose own EnableChannel replaces the flag word, so it cannot exempt a started voice; its other caller 0x6c5630 (execute of the voice-update record, vtable 0x70a850, built on playing channels at 0x6bbb56, 0x6bbcc3, 0x6bbe16, 0x6bc9c1, 0x6bcc4b, 0x6bccb6, 0x6bd766 and 0x6c3d58) sends nothing either: those builders zero the request and only call setters that write the field mask +0x14 (0x6c5130, 0x6c5170, 0x6c50d0), so +0xc stays 0, 1, 0x20 or 0x820 and the test +0xc & 0x1a fails, 0x40 never reaching 0x10 that way; 0x6c5110 (it stores the loop count at +0x44 and ORs 0x40 into the field mask +0x14 of the block it is called on) has four callers: voice flag 4 at 0x6bc626, 0x6be016 and 0x6c3c3c, and 0x6c6511 in 0x6c64f0, slot +0x14 of the two node classes whose vtables start at 0x70a678 and 0x70aba8 (slots 0x70a68c and 0x70abbc): it first passes its argument to the inner node (virtual +0x48, then that node's slot +0x14) and then, when the dword +0x18 of the argument has bit 0x40, hands the argument this node's own loop count [+0x14] through 0x6c5110, so it copies a loop count into a parameter block and can only give a loop's no-Doppler bit; who writes bit 0x40 into a block's +0x18 was not found (a search of the text for a store of 0x40 into a +0x18 field finds only 0x43767e and 0x537278, outside the audio code), so that route stays unread; the volume chain is ported (round 11, review): the voice's volume index is its percent times the float of each group of its mask (master, music, speech, effects: the service's percent x 0.01f stored by 0x6b98d0; start value 1.0 at 0x70a7dc), formed as floats and rounded to even (0x6bb860, SoundVolume.Index); the driver (0x6d2980) sends nothing for an index below 0 or above 127 and otherwise the 16-bit entry of the table 0x782e40 (258 x index, 32767 for the last entry, SoundVolume.Entry); QMixer's message case 2 (0x180045c0, 0x18007a1c) stores entry / 32767 (100 gives 0.7874) as the volume +0x4c and recomputes the product +0x4c x +0xb8 (distance gain; 1 for a flat or unpositioned voice) x +0xb4 (cone; none) = +0xbc, which 0x1800586b hands to 0x1800ca40 (0 at exactly 1.0, -10000 at 0 or less, else round-half-even of 602.0599913279623 x log2) once, and the result is DirectSound's volume for every wave; only SetPan is gated by the format table (0x1800597c-0x1800599f: mono waves). An event-system voice (every voice the sound event system starts, the chain children and sentence segments included) takes that chain in place of the master and channel gains (the groups are inside the index) and a group volume change re-evaluates the index (0x6b98d0 -> record 0x70a610 -> 0x6c56e2 -> 0x6bb860; OpenTPW evaluates it for each mixed block); voices OpenTPW plays outside its event system (the music and the advisor's clip) keep the master and channel gains times their own volume; also open: the stream frequency's rounding to the device's units (0x1800c760); the raised listener of the game pause (docs/AUDIO.md, Pause sound) adds one prediction of the reading to test against the original: the listener's jump of 10000 is the velocity of the next changed update, which for a camera looking down is x <= -1 for a flat voice, so an advisor sentence paused by a game pause and resumed would run at ten times its speed until the camera moved; a coaster save also stores the handle numbers of the train loops (train +0x64 and +0x68, 0x43780A..0x437821, restored raw at 0x438400), which only mean something to the previous session's sound library: OpenTPW starts no loop for a loaded train until its next setup; the handle kept in train +0x68 is used afterwards only as the target of the parameter writes 0x51BC40 (0x4397E0 and 0x439804 in 0x439780), which address whichever sound node of the new session holds that number: not modelled"),
		("AUDIO-038", "the voice pool of 12 (tp.exe 0x6b62b0) ranks the held chains (screams) with the running sentences, placed sounds and one-shots of the event system by priority and ground distance (0x6bb9f0; a started any-distance voice sits at the origin of the sound space, where its sample start put it, 0x6bc51d), a voice entering only while its rank exceeds the pool's lowest (0x6b6bc0) and among equal ranks the newer voice keeping its slot (0x6b6a80 links a new voice at the head of the voice list); a voice out of range without the any-distance flag (event flag 0x200, voice flag 8 at 0x6bca44) has its sample stopped by mixer message 3 (0x6bca70 -> 0x6b8d60 -> 0x6bb7c0) and its handle at +0x24 cleared, is not ticked, and returns by the sample setup 0x6bc410: one-shots, loops and sentence segments restart from their beginning with a fresh volume, pitch and delay draw, a chain starts no new child but its children already playing play out. The group enable is ported (round 11; SoundEventSystem.GroupDisabled, AudioMixer.SetChannelEnabled): the tick 0x6bc760 tests the voice's mask byte +0x47 (the category's byte +0x08, SoundEvent.GroupMask: bits 1 master, 2 music, 4 speech, 8 effects) against the sound system's enable bytes +0x44..+0x47 (0x6b9900; all 1 from 0x6b8f32, the master's never written; the sound service sets music, speech and effects from its on-flags 0x803ADC, 0x803AE0 and 0x803AD8 with the volumes, 0x51BD70, and the on-flags come from the profile's applier 0x423DD0 and a save's Sound block, not from a toggle, which sets the volume to 0, GameAudio.ApplyOnFlags), and a voice with a clear byte has its sample stopped (record 0x70a2d0 with the handle at +0x24, which is cleared) and is not run, as an out-of-range voice: back on, the tick starts it through the sample setup (loops, one-shots and sentence segments from their beginning with fresh draws; a chain makes no child meanwhile and the children it made play out, they are not in the pool); the voice flag 0x4000 the gate sets on events whose flags have 0x4 or 0x8 only keeps the parameter updates (0x6bbbcb) and the container tick (0x6bcb8d) from running. Open: a sentence stopped inside the decoder's last look-ahead resumes on its successor, which this replay of the whole segment does not do: tp.exe's callback 0x6bb4f0 -> 0x6bb510 hands QMixer's decoder one block of 0x2400 bytes (0x6d6db0: the request's +0x20, else 0x2400) from a buffer its worker decoded ahead (0x6bb590, virtual +0x1c = 0x6c6300; a result other than 1 marks the segment's data finished, +0x30) and starts decoding the next block, and QMixer's decoder (0x1801ef10) asks for the next block when its one block is used up, so the refill 0x6bde00 that advances the selected descriptor runs one block before the end of the audible data plus the DirectSound lead of QMixer's mixing, which was not read, and a stop inside that window resumes on the successor (OpenTPW has no block model); the sentence successor is chosen when a segment starts, not when the decoder's data runs out (EXT:sentence-prefetch); the initial submission's admission (the submit 0x6b7f20 builds the voice and executes the record 0x70a580 at once, 0x6b8360 -> 0x6c5690; whether the first sample start waits for a tick was not read); the voices OpenTPW plays outside its event system (the advisor's clip, SpeechAudioPlayer: its group goes silent instead of stopping its sample) and the children a chain makes, which do not compete"),
	};

	private static bool logged;

	public static void LogOnce()
	{
		if ( logged )
			return;
		logged = true;
		foreach ( var (id, rule) in All )
			Log.Trace( $"[APPROX:{id}] {rule}" );
	}
}
